  "grammar": {
    "title": "Informal Imperatives",
    "subtitle": "Telling someone to do something — the way people actually talk at home",
    "explanation": "<h3>Making commands in spoken Farsi</h3><p>When you tell someone to do something at home, you use the <strong>informal imperative</strong> — the simplest verb form in Farsi. You hear it constantly: <em>biyah</em> (come!), <em>boro</em> (go!), <em>beshin</em> (sit!).</p><p>For most verbs, you take the present stem and add <strong>be-</strong> (بـ) at the front:</p>",
    "formation": [
      "Take the present stem of the verb (the short form, like <em>shin</em> from نشستن)",
      "Add <strong>be-</strong> (بـ) to the front → <strong>بشین</strong> <em>beshin</em> (sit!)",
      "For negative commands, use <strong>na-</strong> (نـ) instead → <strong>نشین</strong> <em>nashin</em> (don't sit!)",
      "Some common verbs are irregular: بیا <em>biyah</em> (come!), برو <em>boro</em> (go!), ببین <em>bebin</em> (look!)"
    ],
