  "culture": {
    "title": "Taarof at the Breakfast Table",
    "content": "<p><strong>Taarof</strong> (تعارف) is the art of politeness in Persian culture. You'll hear it everywhere — at the table, at the door, even at the store. It's not just manners; it's a rhythm that every Iranian learns from childhood.</p><p>At breakfast, if Mass offers you tea, the taarof way is to first decline:</p><div class='culture-example'><div class='example-persian'>نه ممنون، زحمت نکش.</div><div class='pronunciation'>nah mamnoon, zahmat nakesh.</div><div>No thanks, don't trouble yourself.</div></div><p>Mass will insist — maybe two or three times. This is normal. When you finally accept, you show gratitude:</p><div class='culture-example'><div class='example-persian'>دستت درد نکنه.</div><div class='pronunciation'>dastet dard nakoneh.</div><div>Literally: \"May your hand not hurt.\" (Thank you for the effort.)</div></div><p>You'll hear <strong>خواهش می‌کنم</strong> (<em>khahesh mee-konam</em>) constantly — it means \"you're welcome\" but literally \"I beg of you.\" That captures the taarof spirit: everything is offered as if the other person is doing you a favor by accepting.</p><p><strong>Tip for Sam:</strong> You don't need to do full taarof with Mass at home — she'll laugh if you refuse tea three times! But notice when she does it with guests or on the phone. It's one of the most beautiful parts of Persian culture.</p>"
  },
